  "subtitle": "Programming a robot by grabbing it — jog buttons, a limp-mode arm, and a record key",
  "why_scout_cares": "Industrial HCI and programming-by-manipulation: the PUMA's handheld 'manual control unit' lets a human jog a six-axis arm in joint or world space, then push it by hand in FREE (limp) mode while the controller records positions into a VAL program. The first widely used robot programming language, taught not by typing but by physically guiding the machine. Nothing in the museum covers industrial robotics or physical programming-by-example.",
  "search_terms": ["Unimation PUMA teach pendant", "VAL programming language 1980", "manual control unit robot", "lead-through programming robot", "PUMA 560"],
